{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact G3 repository-binding source head 7e6dcfd9dcc324a70690b601e68307ea10929fc5 on builder/repository-binding-20261102, for source child d5cc652b under promise 235eb36d and review request a0084aeb. This approval covers the source and the configuration procedure only. It claims no deployed mini, no filled configuration and no acceptance of parent 3c6ec378.\n\nScope. Main 04f4af48 is an ancestor. The head adds seven commits and changes 63 paths. After the frozen source ac8386c7, only the note and the manifest change. The worktree is clean, and the commit-message check passes for all seven commits. Seven live artifacts at the head's paths sit off main, at b779cf01 on request/native-room-naming from 10 October. Each one shares an exact path with an artifact of this head, so the reviewed scope covers its retirement. No card path changes, so no Card workflow applies.\n\nThe native cohort. The opt-in family takes new versions: Register 25, Directory 24, Membership 11, Rules 10, Destination 18 and Inbox 1. Membership 11 adds destination.bind-repository to the admin row of ROLE_TABLE_OF and FIRST_ACTIONS_OF only. It shares Membership 7's declaration text, so the generated file only reorders that existing line. Destination 18's bind-repository requires the unpublished state and a valid GitHub record and branch. It then moves the branch to empty and opens the first-head and mint operations, so a second bind fails. Directory 24 takes repository-bound only from the destination recorded on its repository item, and only while that slot is empty. historical-continuity.json shows no change under packages/platform, the invitation contract, the application route or the Page bundle from b3da6de3 through ac8386c7. The default cohort and every historical pin stay fixed. docs/self-host.md says plainly that earlier rooms, including H3, cannot bind, and it describes the deliberate move to a new room, as e249d290 requires.\n\nOutside authority. A native binding grants nothing outside by itself:\n\n- GITHUB_REPOSITORY_BINDINGS is a closed, bounded list. It allows at most 64 rows and 65,536 bytes, refuses duplicate destinations and duplicate ID-and-branch pairs, and requires the namespace to equal the App account. A malformed list turns the whole GitHub wiring off.\n- For a destination whose definition can bind and whose genesis recorded no repository, bound() requires the full current incarnation, repository record and branch to match one reviewed row. That gate covers effects, snapshots, staging, replies, credentials and recovery.\n- Under installationReads, every other destination and the register are closed.\n\nTokens, against 3cfac8c2 and e54eb718:\n\n- Every mint passes exactly one repository ID.\n- mintInstallationToken in packages/git, unchanged by this head, refuses an empty ID list before any request. It also refuses a response whose selection is not \"selected\", whose permissions differ from the request except the implicit metadata read, or whose repository list holds any other ID.\n- In installationReads mode, #installationRead validates the installation, mints a contents-read token for the one ID, and verifies the repository's name and stable ID before any Git read.\n- Publication mints its own one-ID contents-write token only after #mintIdentity has passed.\n- installationReads requires privateRepositories true and publicReads false, and it refuses a configured GITHUB_READ_TOKEN.\n- Nothing requests the administration permission. The independent-read path stays the default.\n\nThe configuration helper. prepare-github.mjs requires an owned 0700 project whose settings equal the maintained template, and a policy that equals the checked-in pin list. Key and token files must belong to the caller and have mode 0600 or 0400. The helper validates the full configuration with the production parser before any write. It refuses an existing output path, writes 0700 and 0600 files with exclusive create, makes no network call and echoes no input on failure. repository-binding-policy.json holds only the six pins. The repository's fixture keys are placeholders: the deploy test's key decodes to \"TEST\".\n\nThe custody clarification. The builder's reading in f96845d0 matches the code. GitHubProvider.#source mints its Git-read token for each read and keeps it only in memory. Publication and member credentials still go through DestinationHost into CredentialStore's private SQLite table, and this head changes neither credential-store.ts nor destination-host.ts. In installationReads mode, the member credential is the verified one-ID read token. Point 4 of e54eb718, \"tokens live in memory only\", is therefore broader than the code and broader than the custody protocol that earlier reviews accepted. I recommend the planner restate it this way: provider lookup and Git-read tokens stay in memory, and publication and member credentials keep the existing private SQLite custody, outside history and evidence. That restatement belongs to the planner under 3bbf8599. It does not block this source, which adds no custody protocol.\n\nGates and CI. Three earlier gates read \"exit 1\" at 6f86a0c2, 4e982600 and b3da6de3, and each repair changed production or shared code, so each took a new full gate, as docs/testing.md requires. The final gate at ac8386c7 reads exit 0, with matching before and after source guards and a clean status. The terminal output shows 3,577 plain passes with one skip and 1,037 Native passes with two skips. Page browser workflow 41456309428 succeeds at exact ac8386c7.\n\nControls. The named controls each remove one real guard and run the whole owning file:\n\n- foreign register, with a canonical foreign ID;\n- a permissive private-file mode;\n- the operator pair in the independent-read mode and in the App-only mode;\n- the pre-mint identity order;\n- the empty repository-ID scope;\n- the App-only stable-ID check;\n- an ordinary member's binding grant, over real private sessions.\n\nEach exits 0 with its expected test failing and source restored. The first foreign-register control and the first empty-scope and pre-mint attempts stay recorded as inconclusive, with the reason.\n\nEvidence. The manifest hashes to 4eff8f21…5980, as the request states. All 195 files in its 9 units match their sizes and digests, no unit holds an unlisted file, and each unit's SHA256SUMS matches its manifest hash and passes. All 32 output records in the table match their manifest entries. No evidence file is writable, and the scan for private keys, GitHub tokens and bearer values finds no match.\n\nArchitecture: sound. Native state says that a room bound a repository, operator configuration says that this host may act on it, and GitHub's own token response proves the narrowing. Each layer fails closed on its own. Reusing the unpublished birth and first-head protocol keeps one publication path.\n\nSecurity: no blocking finding. The App key on the mini can mint for all 26 installation repositories, and the self-host guide says so and names the one-repository installation as the stronger choice. One limit deserves a line in docs/self-host.md at the next touch: in installationReads mode the register's outside port is closed too, so that host cannot create or delete repositories. G3 needs neither, because the witness repository already exists.\n\nSimplification: no required change. The note says Destination 18 binds only \"without a recorded repository, head or token\". The code enforces that through the unpublished-state guard and the transition to empty, not through separate checks. The native owner's second-bind refusal covers it.\n\nNext steps. The filled App configuration and the binding row each come back as small heads for review before the planner moves the key or deploys, as 9878af36 and e54eb718 set out. The custody sentence waits on the planner.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2ffdec58d64a0472d49032515292146b4c